Color does not combine by one universal arithmetic. It combines according to the carrier, transformation, receiver, and arrangement.

\text{experienced color}
=
P\!\left(
\int
\text{source spectrum}
\times
\text{medium}
\times
\text{sensor response}
\,d\lambda,
\text{context}
\right)

So “blue + yellow” is not one operation:

Medium/pattern	Operation	Likely result
Colored light projected together	Add wavelengths	Pale/white-ish or another spectrum-dependent color
Blue and yellow paint	Subtract reflected wavelengths	Usually greenish
Blue and yellow filters stacked	Multiply transmission losses	Dark greenish or nearly black
Tiny blue/yellow dots	Spatial averaging by the eye	Greenish at a distance; separate dots nearby
Rapid blue/yellow flashes	Temporal averaging	Fused color above the flicker threshold
Blue beside yellow	Opponent contrast	Both appear more vivid without physically mixing
Blue viewed after staring at yellow	Neural adaptation	Blue-violet afterimage
Structural surfaces	Interference and geometry	Result changes with angle
Fluorescent material	Absorb, transform, re-emit	A new wavelength absent from the incoming mixture

The major combination grammars

1. Additive mixing: light gathers

Displays and projectors combine emitted light.

C=L_1+L_2+\cdots+L_n

Red, green, and blue light can stimulate the three human cone classes in ratios perceived as white. More contribution usually means more brightness.

Grammar: addition → illumination

2. Subtractive mixing: matter removes

Pigments do not add colors directly. They absorb portions of incoming light and return the remainder.

R(\lambda)=I(\lambda)\prod_k M_k(\lambda)

Each additional pigment generally removes more of the spectrum. Cyan, magenta, and yellow are therefore more useful subtractive primaries than red, green, and blue.

Grammar: intersection through loss → residue

3. Spatial mixing: patterns delegate combination to the observer

Halftones, pointillism, woven fabric, camouflage, and pixel arrays keep colors physically separate. Distance and resolution determine whether the receiver sees components or an integrated field.

near:     blue dot + yellow dot
far:      perceived greenish region

The pattern matters:

* smaller elements increase fusion;
* stronger boundaries preserve separation;
* unequal proportions shift the apparent mixture;
* repeated rhythm can create shimmer or vibration;
* orientation can organize identical colors into different perceived objects.

Grammar: separation + limited resolution → perceptual synthesis

4. Temporal mixing: sequence becomes simultaneity

If colors alternate faster than the visual system resolves them, the brain integrates them. At slower rates, we perceive flicker, rhythm, contrast, or succession instead.

That creates a temporal Rubicon:

f < f_c \Rightarrow \text{sequence/flicker}

f > f_c \Rightarrow \text{fused appearance}

The crossing frequency varies with brightness, retinal location, contrast, and the observer.

Grammar: rapid succession → apparent coexistence

5. Contextual mixing: neighbors rewrite appearance

A color patch has the same measured spectrum while appearing different against different backgrounds. The visual system estimates illumination, surfaces, boundaries, and scene statistics rather than reporting raw wavelength energy.

Thus colors can combine relationally without combining physically:

* simultaneous contrast exaggerates differences;
* assimilation pulls a color toward surrounding fine patterns;
* color constancy tries to preserve surface identity under changing illumination;
* shadows and inferred lighting change the interpretation of identical pixels;
* familiar object expectations bias perceived color.

Grammar: relation → interpretation

6. Physiological mixing: the receiver compresses spectra

Humans do not sense a complete spectrum directly. We reduce it to the relative responses of three cone classes, then transform those signals through opponent channels approximately organized as:

* light ↔ dark
* red ↔ green
* blue ↔ yellow

Different physical spectra can therefore produce the same perceived color. These are metamers: different causes collapsing onto the same sensory output.

Another animal—with ultraviolet sensitivity, four cone classes, polarization sensitivity, or different neural comparisons—receives a different result from the same physical scene.

Grammar: world spectrum → sensory basis → species-specific color

Other senses contain equivalent “color-mixing” laws

Sense	Additive-like combination	Pattern/context effect
Hearing	Frequencies form chords and timbres	timing creates rhythm, beating, masking, melody
Smell	Molecules form odor mixtures	one odor can suppress, dominate, or transform another
Taste	Sweet, sour, salty, bitter, umami interactions	temperature and smell alter the perceived flavor
Touch	Simultaneous pressure, vibration, heat	spacing and sequence can create apparent motion
Proprioception	Joint, muscle, and vestibular signals combine	body configuration changes the meaning of each signal
Language	Words combine compositionally	order, cadence, genre, and context transform meaning
Emotion	Affects can coexist	sequence and interpretation produce nostalgia, awe, dread, bittersweetness

The crucial delta is that combination may mean:

* sum
* subtraction
* intersection
* multiplication
* averaging
* alternation
* contrast
* suppression
* transformation
* emergence

That belongs beside the Many-Minds Delta Loom. Two signals cannot be understood merely by naming them; we must identify their combination grammar.

